00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.450
So where do the HashiCorp
tools fit into this?

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.080
Today we'll start with Vagrant.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.440
Chris, can you tell us a
little bit about Vagrant

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.037
and what problems it solves?

00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:19.870
CHRIS ROBERTS: Sure.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:22.960
Vagrant is a tool primarily
used by software developers.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.550
It provides an easy
and repeatable way

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.920
to create custom
developer environments.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.580
Vagrant has been
around for a long time

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.288
and brings together
the well known worlds

00:01:30.288 --> 00:01:31.910
of VMs and operating
systems, along

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:33.460
with some of the
same patterns, you

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.170
may have heard
about with Docker,

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.630
but without having to adopt a
new container-centric world.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:38.800
ERIC JOHNSON: Thanks, Chris.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.260
I think we've all heard stories,
or experienced this ourselves,

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.150
of working at places
where developers create

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:44.920
their environments by
copying and pasting

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.550
a bunch of shell commands
from some internal wiki page.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.990
Vagrant essentially replaces
those set up instructions

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:51.700
with a single
configuration file that

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.190
can be put under version
control and treated just

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:55.030
like your code.

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.470
Correct me if I'm
wrong, but a common use

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.030
case is for developers
to use Vagrant

00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.710
to spin up local
VMs if they have

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.812
VirtualBox or VMware installed.

00:02:01.812 --> 00:02:03.270
CHRIS ROBERTS: Yes,
that's correct.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.311
With local VMs, the
developer isn't cluttering up

00:02:05.311 --> 00:02:08.169
their host's operating system
with a bunch of extra software,

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:10.664
or having to run a
specific operating system.

00:02:10.664 --> 00:02:12.580
It's easy to create and
destroy these VMs too.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.280
What can start to
get tricky, though,

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.390
is when developers want to
collaborate using a local VM.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.600
And while Vagrant does
have a share feature,

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.860
that may not always
be a viable option.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.970
SPEAKER 3: So, they can use a
remote VM that they can share.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:28.300
This is where GCP integration
with Vagrant comes in.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.670
Google Cloud Platform has
many different services

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.710
ranging from big data
and machine learning,

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:34.600
all the way down to
infrastructure offerings

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.090
such as our VM service,
Compute Engine.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:39.650
Let's get started with how
developers can use Vagrant

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:43.000
to spin up a shared GCP VM.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.790
CHRIS ROBERTS: First, go to
vagrantup.com and download

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.890
the version for
your local system.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:53.050
SPEAKER 3: Next, go to
cloud.google.com/free-trial

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.270
to create a Google
Cloud Platform account,

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:56.770
if you don't already have one.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.710
Next, you'll need to enable
the Google Compute Engine API.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.340
A service account and a
corresponding JSON credential

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:05.780
file will need to
be created as well.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.660
Now we'll spin up a
Google Compute Engine

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.820
instance with my
hyperspace video game

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.160
and develop and environment.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.460
Ready to go.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.170
Don't forget to install the
Vagrant Google driver using

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.430
Vagrant's plug in command.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:20.470
I'll do that with a
Vagrant up-command

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:22.240
with Google providers specified.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.730
Of course, for Vista work,
I'll need to create my Vagrant

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:25.910
file first.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.727
Chris, can you walk us
through our Vagrant file?

00:03:28.727 --> 00:03:29.560
CHRIS ROBERTS: Sure.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:31.390
First, we define the
provider information

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.820
to access the GCE API.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.790
This includes a project
ID, client email,

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:38.650
and user information.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.110
Next, we provide details
for the type of instance

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:43.600
we want Vagrant to
create and set the SSH

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.190
information to allow access.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:48.070
Using Vagrant sync
folders, we're

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:52.080
able to push the local source
code to the new instance.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:53.570
And finally, a
provisioning script

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:55.790
is used to create
the new instance.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:58.320
Here, a simple shell provisioner
runs an in-line script

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.220
on the remote instance.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:01.950
Vagrant also supports
other provisioners,

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.680
like Chef and Puppet, for more
complex instance configuration.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.250
Now let's see how the GCE
instance is coming along.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.620
SPEAKER 3: Okay, now that
the Vagrant-up has finished,

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:15.450
you can see in the console that
I have a new GCE VM running.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.440
When I click on its
public IP address,

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.140
you can see that the
game is up and working.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:22.760
Vagrant and GCE make a
great combination for us

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.360
in areas like this, when
you have multiple developers

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.250
needing access to the same VM.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:30.190
GCE will store users'
SSH keys and metadata,

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:34.420
and those keys are propagated to
the VM to allow remote access.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.071
CHRIS ROBERTS: Yes, with
a quick Vagrant SSH,

00:04:37.071 --> 00:04:39.070
you can see there are
multiple users in the home

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:43.114
directory, each with their
own SSH key to access the VM.

00:04:43.114 --> 00:04:45.280
You can also see that Arden's
source code was copied

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.040
at the VM, and [? serve ?]
hyperspace directory,

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.180
as was specified in
his Vagrant file.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:52.290
So that was pretty quick--

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.810
almost as fast as
spinning up a local VM.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.820
ERIC JOHNSON: Yeah,
that looked really easy.

